{"id": "section-body-05033", "title": "Third-line Antiretroviral Therapy", "chapterId": "chapter-six", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "MANAGEMENT OF HIV AND AIDS", "Management of Adult and Adolescent Living with HIV with Antiretroviral Therapy", "Third-line Antiretroviral Therapy"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-05033", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Third-line Antiretroviral Therapy", "runs": [{"text": "Third-line"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Antiretroviral"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Therapy"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "1741"}}, {"id": "body-05034", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "RoC failing second line may have substantial NRTI and NNRTI-associated resistance mutations (RAMs), limiting their usage in the third line regimen. Third-line regimens should include at least two, preferably three, effective drugs, constructed using second-generation formulations of preceding antiretrovirals with low or no cross-resistance. These second-generation drugs have a greater genetic barrier to resistance, and their efficacy is rarely jeopardized by RAMs associated with first-generation formulations.", "runs": [{"text": "RoC failing"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "second"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "line"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "may"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "have"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "substantial"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "NRTI"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "NNRTI-associated"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "resistance"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "mutations"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(RAMs),"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "limiting"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "their"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "usage in the third line regimen. Third-line regimens should include at least two, preferably three, effective drugs, constructed using second-generation formulations of preceding antiretrovirals with low or no cross-resistance. These second-generation drugs have a gr